    <Program class="ca.nrcan.gc.OEE.ERS.ErsProgram">
        <Labels>
            <English>EnerGuide Rating System</English>
            <French>Système de cote ÉnerGuide</French>
        </Labels>
        <Version major="15" minor="1" build="19">
            <Labels>
                <English>v15.1b19</English>
                <French>v15.1b19</French>
            </Labels>
        </Version>
        <SdkVersion major="1" minor="11">
            <Labels>
                <English>v1.11</English>
                <French>v1.11</French>
            </Labels>
        </SdkVersion>
        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="false" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="false" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="1">
                    <English>Unknown</English>
                    <French>Inconnu</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Overall, your house has a lot of excess air leakage, with 4.8 air changes per hour at 50Pa. However there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. Air leakage was observed around the exterior doors, as well as at the attic hatch and the crawlspace access. Replacing the weatherstripping around your doors as well as the gasket around the crawlspace access could potentially reduce your air leakage. 



Should you decide to replace some or all of your windows, this will also reduce your air leakage.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $65&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;2.1 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 studs with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation, as well as 2x2 strapping with 1.5 inches of insulation. I have modelled adding 1.5 inches (R8) of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation.



I do not recommend this upgrade unless your siding is approaching the end of its life. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding R8 of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $175&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;5.7 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$1,125 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;

</mainWalls>
                <windows>

All of your windows are older double pane ones that do not appear to have Low E coatings or an insulating gas fill between the panes of glass. In addition, they are all sliding windows, which do not seal as well as hinged windows. I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all of them with ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



In general, your windows are in good shape, and replacing windows has a long payback. 



I did note that the window in your back door has lost its seal. I recommend replacing this door.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing all 11 windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $230&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;7.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

I recommend that you replace your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.87 or higher. A water heaters with an energy factor of 0.92 would qualify you for a rebate. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.87, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $1,070&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs and increase your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;0.8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. If you purchase a water system with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher, you will qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$1,250 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program and increase your energy savings even further. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;

</HotWaterSystem>
                <WaterConservation>

I recommend you replace two toilets, as neither of them is currently a low-flow toilet. When replacing your toilets, purchase 4.8 Liters or less, or dual-flush toilets. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing your toilet(s) with a new 4.8 Liters or less, or dual-flush toilet(s), you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save ~$50 &lt;/b&gt; per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;b&gt;Crawlspace Ventilation&lt;/b&gt;

While I was in your crawlspace, I noticed that it smelled musty and that there was water condensing on various surfaces. I strongly recommend you confirm your sump pump is working properly, and consider getting a contractor to add ventilation to the crawlspace. This will allow your crawlspace to dry out and reduce the risk of structural damage to your home as a result of the moisture. 



&lt;b&gt;Woodstove&lt;/b&gt;

Since you have a wood stove and indicated that you use it, I have calculated your estimated annual savings. Using your woodstove for approximately 50% of your heating load will potentially &lt;b&gt;save $930&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;0.4 GJ&lt;/b&gt; based on our standard fuel costs.



&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Yellowknife&lt;/b&gt;

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.45 min, $0.30 per kWh

Oil  - $0.84 per litre 

Propane - $0.71 per litre

Wood - $341 per cord 

Pellets - $6.58 per bag



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Disclaimer&lt;/b&gt;

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Your Energy Advisor&lt;/b&gt;



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



&lt;b&gt;Alexandra Giroux

Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance&lt;/b&gt;

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: alexandra.giroux@aea.nt.ca



&lt;b&gt;Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!&lt;/b&gt;

</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
        </Options>
